      "id": 1,
      "text": "New Lebanon was founded in 1740 by George Steitz and was originally called Steitztown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lebanon%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from the West Asian country Lebanon, though locals pronounce it “Leb‑nin” or “Lep‑nin.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lebanon%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Chestnut Street Log House, Cornwall & Lebanon Railroad Station, and Salem Evangelical Lutheran Church are listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lebanon%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Lebanon bologna, a cured sausage, was first produced here before the 1780s and remains a local specialty.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lebanon%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Union Canal ran through New Lebanon’s north side from 1828 until it closed in 1885.",
